# Copy to .env.local. That file is gitignored.

# ─── Notion ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required. The integration token that reads and writes the card database.
# Notion: Settings → Connections → your connection → Configuration →
# Integration token. The database id itself is not a secret and lives in
# lib/core/notion.ts; the token is what grants access to it.
#
# The database has to be shared with the connection individually
# (••• → Connections on the database), and the connection needs "Insert
# content" on top of read access or every write is refused while every read
# keeps working, which is the one failure that looks like a bug rather than a
# missing permission.
NOTION_TOKEN=

# ─── Writing ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required for POST /v1/cards and GET /v1/fields. One shared passcode: one
# person edits this collection, so an account system with a password reset
# behind it would be furniture. Any long random string; `openssl rand -hex 24`.
#
# Without it every write answers 503 and says so, which is the intended state
# for a deployment nobody adds cards from.
# The address that signs in, beside CARDS_TOKEN. One account, so this is a
# single value rather than a table; it is also what the login field is filled
# in with, so it is not a secret. Without it nobody can sign in and the form
# says so rather than refusing every password.
OWNER_EMAIL=

CARDS_TOKEN=

# ─── Who may post ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma separated. Browsers on *other* origins may write from here; anything
# else not listed is 403. A request with no Origin header at all is not a
# browser (the iOS app, curl) and is allowed: neither can be tricked into
# posting on someone else's behalf, which is the risk this defends against.
#
# This app's own pages do not need to be listed here, on any domain: guard.ts's
# sameOrigin() compares Origin against the request's own host and allows it
# regardless of this list. Leaving it empty only blocks a *different* site or
# tool from posting here — never this one.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000

# ─── Where this deploys ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The app's own absolute address, used for canonicals, og:url, the sitemap and
# robots.txt. Optional in production: without it, SITE_URL in lib/core/config.ts
# falls back to Vercel's own VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL, then to localhost.
# Set explicitly whenever a custom domain is in front of Vercel, so a canonical
# never points at a *.vercel.app address instead of the real one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ─── Whose collection ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional, both default in lib/core/config.ts. PUBLIC_USERNAME is the name in
# /user/<name>; OWNER_NAME is what that page calls its owner ("Bart's
# collection" rather than "bartdunweg's collection"). One person today, so
# these are env vars rather than a table.
PUBLIC_USERNAME=bartdunweg
OWNER_NAME=Bart
